sian advance  but as the shelling continues  and civilian casualties rise  the horrors of this war  remain devastatingly clear  alexander van naman is at the front  lines of the refugee crisis she's in  levite which has become a hub for  ukrainians fleeing russian attacks  alexandra are refugees still pouring in  tel aviv where you are and what are they  sharing with you about their experiences  people that are arriving here have been  through a lot they are telling us about  the ways how they escaped russian  artillery assaults how they had to hide  in their basements and many of them are  deeply traumatized let's take tatiana  for example i met her here she is from  harkeef and we started talking in  russian because she is from the  russian-speaking part of the country and  she told me about her beautiful hometown  that i also know and then she burst it  into tears because she reminded herself  that parts of the city now are turned  into rubble and she was worrying  sick about her mom who decided to stay  there and she doesn't know whether her  mom is okay or not and as you can see  there are a lot of such stories that we  are hearing and people want to share  them because they want the world to know  what's happening here  do uh i mean you're in la vie which has  turned into a hub for people trying to  make it to safety as best they can can  the city still handle the influx of  refugees  well the more people arrive here the  more difficult it's going to be  according to the mayor the city is being  stretched to its limits already and he  also said today that  it could be that the near future they  will not be able to provide the refugees  with a safe place to stay of course what  people need is food warm clothes  medicines but first and foremost they  need a place to stay they are staying in  hotels they are staying in apartments  but we met a number of people today who  told us that they don't have a place to  stay and that is one of the reasons why  we are here at the railway station that  you can hear see behind me that are  still a lot of people trying to get to  the polish border trying to get to  poland to other european countries to  feel safe there  alexander phenomenon reporting from la  viv in ukraine alexander thank you so  much for your continued coverage  and i'd like to take you now to the  ukrainian capital of kiev to kiera rudig  she's a member of the ukrainian  parliament and leader of the liberal  pro-european hollows party kyra a very  uh warm welcome to dw news on day 13 of  this war i just want to ask you how are  you kobe  hello thank you for having me  well it's 13th day and our cities are  still standing though  the whole world was saying that we will  fail after 24 to 48 hours so i'm like  watch us  i am super devastated and hurted by all  the pain and suffering that i see when  the refugees are fleeing through kiev  when we are taking refugees from the  outskirts of kiev that were bombarded by  the russian  missiles and bombs  i am super frustrated when we see  children in bomb shelters but i'm also  motivated because i see my resistance  team training because i see so many  people in kiev in the whole country  helping each other and we are getting  ready to give russian forces that are  coming to the capital and will come here  pretty soon we are happy to give them a  good fight  because what they are trying to take  from us they will not be able to we are  preparing for a siege we are preparing  for a fight we are preparing for every  uh kind of event however there is one  thing that we cannot help ourselves with  we cannot use our rifles for is the air  force attacks and this is why we  continuously asking for a no-fly zone or  anything in particular similar like the  no-fly zone so we can protect ourselves  and so that my country has a chance to  win this war  now unfortunately of course nato  says they will not be able to provide  that that no-fly zone is a no-go for  nato i want to refer to  